\begin{abstract}
The Proton--Laser Interaction \& Cosmic Data Integration Engine (v1.2) is a hybrid astrophysical--plasma simulation architecture integrating cosmic dataset ingestion, proton--laser acceleration physics, particle-in-cell (PIC) solvers, magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) coupling, GPU-accelerated computation, and zero-trust encryption. The system models proton acceleration mechanisms including target-normal sheath acceleration (TNSA) and radiation-pressure acceleration (RPA), simulates plasma evolution using Vlasov--Maxwell PIC solvers, and incorporates cosmic boundary conditions derived from astrophysical datasets. This manuscript presents the architecture, physics foundations, computational methodology, and stability governance underlying the engine.
\end{abstract}

\section*{Keywords}
Proton acceleration; laser--plasma interaction; PIC simulation; MHD coupling; astrophysical datasets; GPU acceleration; encrypted scientific computation.

\section{Introduction}
High-energy proton acceleration driven by ultra-intense laser pulses is a central topic in plasma physics, astrophysics, and advanced propulsion research. Modeling these interactions requires coupling laser-driven plasma dynamics with cosmic environmental conditions and high-performance computational frameworks.

The Proton--Laser Interaction \& Cosmic Data Integration Engine (v1.2) introduces a unified architecture combining cosmic dataset ingestion, proton--laser interaction physics, PIC/MHD hybrid solvers, GPU-accelerated computation, and zero-trust encryption. This manuscript provides a formal description of the system architecture and computational methodology.

\section{System Architecture}
The engine consists of five principal subsystems:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Cosmic Data Ingestion \& Preprocessing
    \item Proton--Laser Interaction Physics
    \item PIC/MHD Hybrid Solver
    \item Encryption \& Security Subsystem
    \item HPC Infrastructure \& GPU Acceleration
\end{enumerate}
These subsystems operate under encrypted, topology-consistent scientific computation.

\section{Cosmic Data Ingestion \& Preprocessing}
\subsection{Data Sources}
The ingestion layer supports:
\begin{itemize}
    \item HDF5 astrophysics datasets
    \item FITS telescope data
    \item GEANT4 particle-interaction outputs
    \item Cosmic ray spectra
    \item Star formation models
    \item Interstellar medium (ISM) density maps
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Preprocessing Pipeline}
The pipeline performs coordinate normalization, spectral calibration, cosmic boundary-condition extraction, ISM density field mapping, and proton--laser configuration loading. Laser configuration parameters include pulse duration (fs), intensity (W/cm$^2$), wavelength ($\mu$m), and focal spot size ($\mu$m). This subsystem constructs the cosmic--plasma initial state.

\section{Proton--Laser Interaction Physics}
\subsection{Interaction Zone}
The engine models laser beam propagation, proton bunch initialization, plasma sheath formation, TNSA acceleration, and RPA acceleration.

\subsection{Governing Equations}
Proton dynamics follow:
\[
\frac{dp}{dt} = q(E + v \times B).
\]
Laser--plasma coupling uses Maxwell's equations:
\[
\nabla \times E = -\frac{\partial B}{\partial t}, \quad
\nabla \times B = \mu_0 J + \mu_0 \epsilon_0 \frac{\partial E}{\partial t}.
\]

\subsection{Plasma Density Evolution}
\[
\Delta n = \Psi(p, E, B).
\]
This subsystem defines the high-energy proton acceleration physics.

\section{PIC/MHD Hybrid Solver}
\subsection{PIC\_SOLVER C++ Module}
The PIC\_SOLVER module includes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Vlasov--Maxwell solver
    \item Particle pusher (Boris/Vay)
    \item Field interpolator (Esirkepov/Yee)
    \item Collision operator
    \item MPI/OpenMP scheduler
\end{itemize}

\subsection{MHD Coupling}
MHD coupling models magnetic field evolution, plasma--field feedback, shock formation, and turbulence dynamics.

\subsection{GPU Acceleration}
GPU acceleration is provided via CUDA/HIP kernels, multi-GPU scaling, and memory-optimized field solvers. This subsystem produces high-fidelity plasma evolution.

\section{Encryption \& Security Subsystem}
\subsection{Encryption}
The engine applies AES-256 data-at-rest encryption, TLS 1.3 data-in-transit encryption, and integrates with hardware security modules (HSMs).

\subsection{Access Control}
Access control is enforced via role-based access control (RBAC) and audit logging (LOO, LSS, SECTION, PREATION).

\subsection{Integrity Verification}
Integrity is verified using Merkle tree hashing and zero-trust compliance, ensuring secure scientific computation.

\section{HPC Infrastructure \& GPU Acceleration}
\subsection{Compute Resources}
The infrastructure layer includes CPU/GPU nodes, Lustre/GPUdirect storage, and InfiniBand interconnects.

\subsection{Workflow Management}
Workflow management is handled via SLURM/PBS job orchestration, real-time data streams, and cluster-wide monitoring, providing petascale computational throughput.

\section{Methods}
\subsection{Data Preparation}
Cosmic datasets are normalized, calibrated, and converted into solver-ready fields.

\subsection{Proton--Laser Initialization}
Laser parameters and proton bunch configurations are loaded into the interaction zone.

\subsection{PIC/MHD Evolution}
Plasma states are evolved using encrypted Vlasov--Maxwell PIC loops with MHD coupling.

\subsection{Encryption Enforcement}
All state transitions are encrypted and validated using Merkle-based integrity signatures.

\subsection{HPC Execution}
Simulations are executed across multi-GPU nodes with memory-optimized solvers.

